<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">The Atelier</p>
  <h1>Artwork</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">Images made for the order. Each one carries a note on what it is claiming — and what it is not.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p>An order that studies symbol had better take its own imagery seriously. Everything here was made for
    EGregoRA: some of it is emblem in the seventeenth-century sense — a picture built to be read rather than
    merely looked at — and some of it is landscape, which argues by atmosphere instead.</p>
    <p class="muted">House rule, applied to pictures as strictly as to claims: an image may be beautiful,
    evocative and entirely symbolic. It may not quietly pose as evidence. Where a plate depicts something real,
    the caption says so. Where it depicts an idea, the caption says that instead.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap info-grid">
  <div class="grid two">

    <figure class="frame reveal">
      <img src="/assets/img/art-tree-of-stars.jpg" alt="A golden Flower of Life mandala over a starfield, with an oak whose branches become constellations" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">
      <figcaption><strong>The Tree of Stars.</strong> The oak and the constellation drawn as one figure —
      the order's founding intuition in a single image: that the thing overhead and the thing in the field are
      continuous, and that only our departments say otherwise.
      <span class="myth">◆ Emblem.</span> No claim is made that branching in trees and filamentary structure in
      the cosmos share a cause; they arise from quite different physics, and the resemblance is the point of the
      picture rather than evidence for anything.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="frame reveal">
      <img src="/assets/img/art-inner-cosmos.jpg" alt="A seated meditating figure whose head opens into a spiral galaxy, ringed with sacred geometry and neural traces" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">
      <figcaption><strong>The Inner Cosmos.</strong> For Limb V. The observer as part of the observed —
      a nervous system modelling a universe that produced it, with the geometry of the model and the geometry of
      the thing drawn in the same gold.
      <span class="myth">◆ Emblem</span>, and a deliberately double-edged one: the visionary state does not grant
      access to the cosmos. It alters the instrument. Both halves of that sentence are in the picture.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="frame reveal">
      <img src="/assets/img/art-ouroboros-elements.jpg" alt="An alchemical plate: ouroboros encircling the four elements with four tinted orbs for the stages of the opus" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">
      <figcaption><strong>Solve et Coagula.</strong> For Limb X, in the manner of the emblem books —
      Maier's <em>Atalanta Fugiens</em> and the <em>Mutus Liber</em>, which taught by picture because the
      pictures were the instruction.
      <span class="hist">◆ Scholarship in form</span>, <span class="myth">◆ myth in content</span>: the four
      elements as states and tendencies, the four stages of the opus as the arc of coming apart and
      reassembling. Nothing here is a recipe.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="frame reveal">
      <img src="/assets/img/art-solstice-circle.jpg" alt="A stone circle on a misty moor at winter solstice dawn with a gold sunbeam aligned between two stones" loading="lazy" width="1024" height="1024">
      <figcaption><strong>Solstice Alignment.</strong> For Limb II. The one picture here depicting something
      literally true: megalithic monuments really are aligned to solar events, and at Newgrange and Maeshowe the
      light really does enter the chamber on the solstice.
      <span class="sci">◆ Archaeoastronomy.</span> What those builders believed is unrecoverable; that they
      measured the sky with extraordinary care is not in doubt.</figcaption>
    </figure>

  </div>
</section>

